<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma'oz Tzur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century, or between <a href="/years/1160">1160</a> and <a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as מָעוֹז צוּר</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Ma'oz Tzur&quot; is a piyyut, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> liturgical poem, sung on the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a> after the festival lights are lit. It takes its name from its opening words, &quot;Strong Rock (of my Salvation)&quot;, a name for God in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. The first letters of its first five stanzas spell the poet's name, Mordechai.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Because of its simple metre the hymn can be sung to almost any tune: congregations use seasonal ones, such as <b>Ma'oz Tzur</b> before Hanukkah, and Hebrew schools and summer camps sing it for fun to &quot;Yankee Doodle&quot; or &quot;Jamaica Farewell&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Ma'oz Tzur</b>&quot; is a piyyut, a Hebrew liturgical poem, sung on the festival of Hanukkah after the festival lights are lit.</blockquote><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a></li></ul></section>
